      "dossier_row_sha256": "87186a9d6e520f65a8f1552bfef5fa064c1289530ee52583734b2426ab27fa57",
      "source_lines": ["src/util/test/vfs.c:122-136"],
      "coverage_difference": "The original resizeMemChunk test checks size and cursor after one growth and shrink, but does not check whether a byte hidden by shrink is exposed again after regrowth within retained backing capacity."
    },
    "source_contract": "VFileMemChunk installs _vfmTruncate, which calls _vfmExpand and clamps the cursor after changing logical size. _vfmExpand reallocates only when toPow2(newSize) exceeds bufferSize. For shrink/regrow within existing capacity it changes vfm->size without clearing backing bytes. During a growth that requires reallocation, the new calloc-backed allocation is zeroed and old logical bytes are copied before the old backing is freed.",
    "caller_trace": {
      "api": "include/mgba-util/vfs.h:83-85",
      "constructor_and_callback_binding": "src/util/vfs/vfs-mem.c:90-118",
      "resize_and_ownership": "src/util/vfs/vfs-mem.c:121-152,290-296",
      "test_caller": "tests/mgba_vfile_memchunk_resize_lifetime.c calls the public constructor, truncate/write/map/close callbacks.",
      "production_callsite": {
        "source": "src/gba/core.c:1493-1506",
        "contract": "_GBACoreSavedataRestore constructs a VFileMemChunk from caller-provided SRAM bytes; the actual GBA restore caller and any production shrink/regrow sequence are not executed here."
      }
    },
    "finding": "On the tested Linux host and four-byte seed, bytes newly exposed by allocation-backed growth are zero; after writing 0xE7, truncating logical size from five to three, and regrowing to five without exceeding the retained eight-byte capacity, the byte at offset four is observable as 0xE7 again. This characterizes retained backing bytes, not a security or gameplay impact."
  },
